---
import ClusterMap from './ClusterMap.astro';
import { actors, flows, faults, gatePresets, type View } from './api-server-data';
interface Props { view: View; }
const { view } = Astro.props;
const capabilities = [
  { group:'Fundamental', items:[
    ['Versioned API and discovery','Clients discover groups such as /api/v1 and /apis/apps/v1 before working with objects.','/api/v1\n/apis/apps/v1\n/apis/metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1\n/apis/example.io/v1'],
    ['Defaulting, conversion and validation','A versioned request becomes a valid object before accepted state is stored.','# Missing required field is rejected:\nPATCH Pod/web-a → 422 Unprocessable\n# Default inserted by API server:\nspec.restartPolicy: Always'],
  ]},
  { group:'Security', items:[
    ['Authentication and authorization','The API identifies a caller and checks the requested verb, resource and scope.','# 401 Unauthorized: bad token\n# 403 Forbidden: RBAC miss\nverb: create\nresource: deployments.apps\nnamespace: demo'],
    ['Admission and webhooks','Applicable writes can be mutated or denied; a matched webhook is an API-server-initiated call.','# Mutating webhook adds a label\n# Validating webhook denies invalid policy\nwebhook → 403 Forbidden\nfailurePolicy: Fail | Ignore'],
  ]},
  { group:'State', items:[
    ['Storage interface and etcd','Durable backing state sits behind API serving; ordinary Kubernetes clients do not use etcd as their API.','# Clients go through the API:\nkubectl → API server → etcd\n# NOT:\nkubectl → etcd (direct)'],
    ['LIST/WATCH and watch cache','Clients follow changes through API watches; serving caches mean every read is not a physical etcd request.','LIST resourceVersion: 10245\nWATCH ?resourceVersion=10245\n→ MODIFIED 11020\n# 410 Gone → re-LIST'],
  ]},
  { group:'Performance', items:[
    ['API Priority and Fairness','Requests can be classified into priority levels and queued or rejected under pressure.','# High priority: system leader election\n# Default priority: user workloads\n# Exempt: health-check paths\nflowSchema → priorityLevelConfig'],
  ]},
  { group:'Extensibility', items:[
    ['Aggregation and APIService','Registered extension API paths can be proxied to an aggregated server.','# Registered aggregated server:\napiVersion: apiregistration.k8s.io/v1\nkind: APIService\nspec:\n  service: {name: metrics-server}'],
    ['Subresources','/status, /scale, /log, /exec and /portforward have distinct purposes and authorization.','# Subresource calls:\nPATCH pods/web-a/status\nGET  pods/web-a/log\nPOST pods/web-a/exec\nPOST pods/web-a/portforward'],
  ]},
  { group:'Operations', items:[
    ['Audit trail','Request stages can generate policy-controlled audit records.','stage: RequestReceived | ResponseStarted | ResponseComplete\nlevel: None | Metadata | Request | RequestResponse'],
    ['Control-plane-to-node proxy','Pod logs, attach and port-forward use special API-server-to-kubelet paths.','# API server initiates call to kubelet:\nGET /api/v1/namespaces/demo/pods/web-a/log\n→ kubelet serves log stream'],
  ]},
];
---
<ClusterMap mapId={view.id} depth={view.id==='responsibilities'||view.id==='conversations'||view.id==='failures'||view.id==='ha-state'?'internals':view.id==='control-data'?'practitioner':'foundation'} />

{view.id==='exchange' && <div class="api-view-fixture"><strong>Start with kubectl. Then inspect another client.</strong><p>Try controller manager, scheduler, kubelet or an extension. Each uses the API for the state it owns; etcd remains behind the API server.</p><div class="api-myth-line"><span>LIKELY MODEL</span><del>kubectl → etcd or controller → kubelet</del><span>ACTUAL MODEL</span><strong>client / controller / kubelet ↔ Kubernetes API state</strong></div></div>}

{view.id==='request-gates' && <div class="api-gate-lab" data-api-gate-lab>
  <div class="api-gate-head"><div role="group" aria-label="API request type"><button type="button" data-gate-mode="write" aria-pressed="true">WRITE · CREATE</button><button type="button" data-gate-mode="read" aria-pressed="false">READ · GET/LIST/WATCH</button></div><div class="api-gate-prompt"><div class="api-gate-cue"><span>TRY A FAILURE</span><strong>Change where the request stops</strong><small>The blocked gate and skipped work update below.</small><b aria-hidden="true">↘</b></div><label><span>Failure scenario</span><span class="api-select-wrap"><select data-gate-preset>{gatePresets.map((preset)=><option value={preset.id}>{preset.label}</option>)}</select></span></label></div></div>
  <ol class="api-gate-rail" data-gate-rail><li data-gate="0"><strong>01</strong><span>TLS / API route</span><small>REQUEST</small></li><li data-gate="1"><strong>02</strong><span>Authentication</span><small>WHO?</small></li><li data-gate="2"><strong>03</strong><span>Authorization</span><small>MAY?</small></li><li data-gate="3"><strong>04</strong><span>Mutating admission</span><small>CHANGE?</small></li><li data-gate="4"><strong>05</strong><span>Validating admission</span><small>ALLOW?</small></li><li data-gate="5"><strong>06</strong><span>Resource processing</span><small>VALID?</small></li><li data-gate="6"><strong>07</strong><span>Persistence</span><small>COMMIT</small></li></ol>
  <div class="api-gate-result" data-gate-result aria-live="polite"><strong>WRITE REQUEST · STEP 1</strong><p>Request enters the API route. No Deployment is stored yet.</p></div><div class="api-lab-controls"><button type="button" data-gate-reset>Reset</button><button type="button" data-gate-prev aria-label="Previous request gate">← Gate</button><button type="button" data-gate-play>▶ Play gates</button><button type="button" data-gate-next aria-label="Next request gate">Gate →</button></div><p class="api-context-note">Teaching pipeline: authentication → authorization → applicable admission → persistence. Ordinary reads bypass admission; a rejected write never reaches etcd.</p>
</div>}

{view.id==='watch' && <div class="api-watch-lab" data-api-watch-lab><div class="watch-before"><span>NAIVE POLLING MODEL</span><del>Controller asks again and again: “anything changed?”</del></div><ol class="api-watch-rail"><li data-watch-step="0"><strong>01 LIST</strong><span>Deployment/demo/web · resourceVersion 10245</span></li><li data-watch-step="1"><strong>02 WATCH</strong><span>Keep a change stream from that version</span></li><li data-watch-step="2"><strong>03 EVENT</strong><span>MODIFIED · resourceVersion 11020</span></li><li data-watch-step="3"><strong>04 RECONCILE</strong><span>Queue demo/web · compare desired with observed</span></li></ol><div class="api-lab-controls"><button type="button" data-watch-prev>← Previous</button><button type="button" data-watch-next>Next →</button><button type="button" data-watch-reset>Reset watch</button></div><details><summary>Show client internals</summary><p>Reflector → local informer cache → handlers → work queue → Reconcile(). The cache can be temporarily behind API state; a logical decision need not issue another GET.</p></details><details><summary>Show server internals</summary><p>API handlers, watch cache and storage interface sit behind the served API. A WATCH event should not be drawn as a separate physical etcd request every time.</p></details><p class="api-context-note">If historical changes are unavailable, `410 Gone` tells a client to clear/re-LIST and restart its watch.</p></div>}

{view.id==='spec-status' && <div class="api-state-lab" data-api-state-lab>
  <div class="api-object-chain" aria-label="Deployment owns ReplicaSet, which owns three Pods">
    <div><span>DESIRED OBJECT</span><strong>Deployment/web</strong><small>replicas: 3 · generation: 1</small></div><b aria-hidden="true">owner / selector →</b>
    <div><span>CONTROLLER OBJECT</span><strong>ReplicaSet/web-7c9</strong><small>owner: Deployment/web</small></div><b aria-hidden="true">owner / selector →</b>
    <div><span>EXECUTION OBJECTS</span><strong data-state-pods>web-a · web-b · web-c</strong><small data-state-identity>3 existing · 3 Ready</small></div>
  </div>
  <div class="api-state-bars"><div><span>DEPLOYMENT SPEC</span><strong>3 desired</strong><meter min="0" max="3" value="3">3 desired</meter></div><div><span>REPLICASET OBSERVATION</span><strong data-state-existing>3 existing</strong><meter data-state-existing-meter min="0" max="3" value="3">3 existing</meter></div><div><span>SERVICE ELIGIBILITY</span><strong data-state-ready>3 ready</strong><meter data-state-meter min="0" max="3" value="3">3 ready</meter></div></div>
  <div class="api-reconcile-rail"><span>OBSERVE OBJECTS</span><span>COMPARE RELATIONSHIP</span><span>ACT IF NEEDED</span><span>↺ OBSERVE AGAIN</span></div>
  <div class="api-version-choices" role="group" aria-label="Change the observed workload state"><button type="button" data-reconcile-case="stable" aria-pressed="true">Stable</button><button type="button" data-reconcile-case="container">Crash container</button><button type="button" data-reconcile-case="delete">Delete Pod</button><button type="button" data-reconcile-case="paused">Pause controller</button></div>
  <div class="api-state-decision" aria-live="polite"><span data-state-owner>REPLICASET CONTROLLER</span><strong data-state-decision>No write needed</strong><p data-state-result>Desired count and existing active Pods match. Reconciliation can finish without changing API state.</p></div>
  <pre data-pod-yaml>{'Deployment/web → ReplicaSet/web-7c9 → Pods [web-a, web-b, web-c]\ndesired: 3 · existing: 3 · ready: 3'}</pre>
  <p class="api-context-note"><strong>Identity boundary:</strong> restarting a container keeps the same Pod UID. Deleting a managed Pod removes that object, so its controller creates a replacement with a new name and UID.</p>
</div>}

{view.id==='flow-lab' && <div class="api-flow-lab" data-api-flow-lab><div class="api-flow-head"><div class="api-flow-picker"><div class="api-flow-cue"><span>CHOOSE A CONVERSATION</span><strong>Follow a different path through the cluster</strong><small>The highlighted actors, object state and steps update together.</small><b aria-hidden="true">↘</b></div><label><span>Cluster conversation</span><span class="api-select-wrap"><select data-flow-select>{(['foundation','practitioner','internals'] as const).map((tier)=><optgroup label={tier==='foundation'?'Foundation':tier==='practitioner'?'Practitioner':'Internals'}>{flows.filter((flow)=>flow.group===tier).map((flow)=><option value={flow.id}>{flow.title}</option>)}</optgroup>)}</select></span></label></div><div class="api-flow-focus"><span>SELECTED EXPLANATION</span><strong data-flow-title>Create a Deployment</strong><p data-flow-question>Does applying replicas: 3 mean three containers exist already?</p></div></div><div class="api-flow-ledger" aria-label="Accumulated lifecycle state"><div><span>OBJECT</span><strong data-flow-ledger-object>Deployment/web</strong></div><div><span>ACCEPTED STATE</span><strong data-flow-ledger-state>request received</strong></div><div><span>DESIRED</span><strong data-flow-ledger-desired>3</strong></div><div><span>READY</span><strong data-flow-ledger-ready>0</strong></div></div><ol class="api-flow-timeline" data-flow-timeline aria-label="Selected flow steps">{flows[0].steps.map((step,i)=><li><button type="button" data-flow-step={i} aria-current={i===0?'step':undefined}><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span>{step.label}</button></li>)}</ol><div class="api-lab-controls"><button type="button" data-flow-first title="First step">|←</button><button type="button" data-flow-prev title="Previous step">←</button><button type="button" data-flow-play>▶ Play Deployment</button><button type="button" data-flow-next title="Next step">→</button><button type="button" data-flow-last title="Final step">→|</button><label>Speed <select data-flow-speed><option value="2">0.5×</option><option value="1" selected>1×</option><option value="0.5">2×</option></select></label><button type="button" data-flow-reset>Reset</button></div><div class="api-flow-step-summary" data-flow-summary aria-live="polite"><strong>01 · Apply</strong><p>Alice sends desired replicas: 3 through the Kubernetes API.</p></div><details open><summary>Current API operation and object fields</summary><div><code data-flow-operation>CREATE Deployment</code><pre data-flow-yaml>{flows[0].steps[0].yaml}</pre></div></details><p class="api-context-note">The actors stay put while the selected operation and accumulated object state change. Deployment playback is timed; every conversation supports direct manual inspection.</p><div class="api-flow-static" aria-label="All flow reading paths without JavaScript"><h3>All cluster conversations</h3>{flows.map((flow,i)=><details><summary>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')} · {flow.title}</summary><p>{flow.question} {flow.takeaway}</p><ol>{flow.steps.map((step)=><li><strong>{step.label} · {step.operation}</strong> — {step.detail}</li>)}</ol><p>{flow.caveat}</p><p>{flow.sources.map((url)=><a href={url}>Primary reference ↗ </a>)}</p></details>)}</div></div>}

{view.id==='placement' && <div class="api-placement-lab" data-api-placement-lab>
  <div class="api-placement-inputs"><label>Pod CPU request<select data-place-cpu><option value="500">500m</option><option value="3000">3000m</option></select></label><label><input type="checkbox" data-place-toleration /> tolerate <code>dedicated=batch</code></label><label><input type="checkbox" data-place-image checked /> image is available</label><label><input type="checkbox" data-place-ready checked /> readiness probe passes</label></div>
  <div class="api-candidate-grid" aria-label="Scheduler candidates"><article data-place-node="a"><span>worker-a</span><strong>2 CPU free</strong><small>no blocking taint</small><b data-place-verdict="a">FEASIBLE</b></article><article data-place-node="b"><span>worker-b</span><strong>4 CPU free</strong><small>taint: dedicated=batch</small><b data-place-verdict="b">FILTERED · TAINT</b></article></div>
  <div class="api-placement-pipeline"><div data-place-stage="object"><span>01</span><strong>Pod object</strong><small>Pending · nodeName unset</small></div><div data-place-stage="schedule"><span>02</span><strong>Schedule</strong><small data-place-schedule>worker-a fits</small></div><div data-place-stage="prepare"><span>03</span><strong>Prepare</strong><small data-place-prepare>image and sandbox ready</small></div><div data-place-stage="run"><span>04</span><strong>Run</strong><small data-place-run>container Running</small></div><div data-place-stage="ready"><span>05</span><strong>Ready</strong><small data-place-ready-copy>eligible endpoint</small></div></div>
  <div class="api-placement-result" aria-live="polite"><span data-place-state>RUNNING · READY</span><strong data-place-title>Scheduled to worker-a and eligible for Service traffic</strong><p data-place-explanation>Scheduler records the binding. Kubelet starts the container and reports readiness separately.</p><pre data-place-evidence>{'kubectl get pod web-a -o wide\nweb-a   1/1   Running   0   worker-a\n\nPodScheduled=True · Ready=True'}</pre></div>
  <p class="api-context-note">This fixture isolates four inputs. Real scheduling evaluates more plugins and node execution can also wait on CNI, CSI, Secrets, init containers and runtime health.</p>
</div>}

{view.id==='conversations' && <div class="api-conversation-lab"><div class="api-map-filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter the cluster map"><strong>FILTER ACTORS</strong>{[['humans','Humans'],['control','Control plane'],['nodes','Nodes'],['networking','Networking'],['storage','Storage'],['autoscaling','Autoscaling'],['extensions','Extensions'],['external','External']].map(([id,label])=><button type="button" data-map-filter={id} aria-pressed="false">{label}</button>)}<button type="button" data-map-filter="all" aria-pressed="true">All</button></div><div class="api-actor-shortlist"><strong>Try a component</strong>{actors.filter((actor)=>['api','controller','scheduler','node-a','proxy','csi','cloud','operator'].includes(actor.id)).map((actor)=><button type="button" data-actor-shortcut={actor.id}>{actor.label}</button>)}</div><p>The controller manager hosts multiple reconciliation loops: Deployment, ReplicaSet, Node lifecycle, EndpointSlice, HPA and others — each independently observes and acts through API state. A kubelet's scope is its own Node; it never instructs other Nodes or issues commands to controllers. Inspect each actor to see the exact read/watch/write boundary it owns.</p><details><summary>Read the component relationship inventory</summary><dl>{actors.map((actor)=><div><dt>{actor.label}</dt><dd><strong>READS:</strong> {actor.reads}. <strong>WATCHES:</strong> {actor.watches}. <strong>WRITES:</strong> {actor.writes}. <strong>WHY:</strong> {actor.why}.{actor.external && <> <strong>EXTERNAL CALL:</strong> {actor.external}.</>}</dd></div>)}</dl></details></div>}

{view.id==='control-data' && <div class="api-dataplane-lab" data-api-dataplane-lab><fieldset><legend>Highlight a path</legend><label><input type="radio" name="api-plane" value="control" checked />Control plane</label><label><input type="radio" name="api-plane" value="data" />Data plane</label><label><input type="radio" name="api-plane" value="both" />Both</label></fieldset><div class="api-plane-rails"><div><span>CONTROL STATE</span><strong>EndpointSlice controller → API → Service proxy</strong><small>WATCH Service app=web · WATCH Pods · CREATE EndpointSlice · backends: 10.1.0.5 · .8 · .9 port 80</small></div><div><span>APPLICATION DATA</span><strong>Client HTTP → Service VIP → forwarding rules → web Pod</strong><small>No ordinary HTTP hop through kube-apiserver · kube-proxy is optional when another implementation exists</small></div></div><p data-plane-result aria-live="polite">Control-plane API state tells a node dataplane which backend addresses are eligible. The API server does not relay application HTTP packets.</p></div>}

{view.id==='failures' && <div class="api-failure-lab" data-api-failure-lab><label>Fail one component or dependency <select data-fault-select>{faults.map((fault)=><option value={fault.id}>{fault.label}</option>)}</select></label><ol class="api-fault-timeline" data-fault-timeline>{faults[0].steps.map((step,i)=><li><button type="button" data-fault-step={i} aria-current={i===0?'step':undefined}><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span>{step.label}</button></li>)}</ol><div class="api-lab-controls"><button type="button" data-fault-first>|←</button><button type="button" data-fault-prev>← Previous</button><button type="button" data-fault-play>▶ Play fault</button><button type="button" data-fault-next>Next →</button><button type="button" data-fault-reset>Reset fault</button></div><div class="api-fault-outcome" aria-live="polite"><strong data-fault-title>Crash one container</strong><dl><div><dt>STOPS</dt><dd data-fault-field="stops">{faults[0].stops}</dd></div><div><dt>CONTINUES</dt><dd data-fault-field="continues">{faults[0].continues}</dd></div><div><dt>FIRST DETECTOR</dt><dd data-fault-field="detector">{faults[0].detector}</dd></div><div><dt>API OBJECT CHANGE</dt><dd data-fault-field="object">{faults[0].object}</dd></div><div><dt>REACTING LOOP</dt><dd data-fault-field="reactor">{faults[0].reactor}</dd></div><div><dt>WORKLOAD TRAFFIC</dt><dd data-fault-field="traffic">{faults[0].traffic}</dd></div><div><dt>RECOVERY CONDITION</dt><dd data-fault-field="recovery">{faults[0].recovery}</dd></div></dl><div class="api-fault-evidence"><span>WHAT TO INSPECT</span><pre data-fault-evidence>{'kubectl get pod web-b\nkubectl describe pod web-b\n# restartCount rises; Pod UID stays the same'}</pre></div></div><p data-fault-summary aria-live="polite">The container exits; kubelet handles the restart inside the same Pod.</p><details class="api-fault-static"><summary>Read all failure outcomes</summary>{faults.map((fault)=><div><h3>{fault.label}</h3><p><strong>Stops:</strong> {fault.stops}. <strong>Continues:</strong> {fault.continues}. <strong>Detector:</strong> {fault.detector}. <strong>API object:</strong> {fault.object}. <strong>Reactor:</strong> {fault.reactor}. <strong>Traffic:</strong> {fault.traffic}. <strong>Recovery:</strong> {fault.recovery}.</p></div>)}</details></div>}

{view.id==='ha-state' && <div class="api-ha-lab" data-api-ha-lab><div class="api-ha-route"><span>CONTROL-PLANE ENDPOINT</span><strong>Load balancer / VIP</strong><span>↓ ROUTE TO SERVING REPLICA</span><div><b data-ha-replica="1">API1 · SERVING</b><b data-ha-replica="2">API2 · SERVING</b><b data-ha-replica="3">API3 · SERVING</b></div><span>↓ DURABLE BACKING</span><strong data-ha-etcd>etcd · HEALTHY</strong></div><div class="api-ha-choices" role="group" aria-label="API high availability state"><button type="button" data-ha-choice="healthy" aria-pressed="true">All healthy</button><button type="button" data-ha-choice="replica" aria-pressed="false">Fail API2</button><button type="button" data-ha-choice="endpoint" aria-pressed="false">Fail endpoint</button><button type="button" data-ha-choice="etcd" aria-pressed="false">Degrade etcd</button></div><p data-ha-result aria-live="polite">API1, API2 and API3 can serve requests concurrently. Controller-manager and scheduler leader election use separate Leases.</p><div class="api-lease-note"><strong>SEPARATE LEASE COORDINATION</strong><span>Controller-manager A active · B standby</span><span>Scheduler A active · B standby</span><span>Node Lease renewals report liveness, not API-server serving leadership.</span></div></div>}

{view.id==='responsibilities' && <div class="api-responsibility-lab"><div class="api-capability-list">{capabilities.map((category,categoryIndex)=><details open={categoryIndex===0}><summary>{category.group}</summary><div>{category.items.map(([title,copy,example])=><button type="button" data-capability-title={title} data-capability-copy={copy} data-capability-example={example} aria-pressed={title==='Versioned API and discovery'?'true':'false'}>{title}</button>)}</div></details>)}</div><div class="api-capability-inspector" aria-live="polite"><strong data-capability-active>Versioned API and discovery</strong><p data-capability-description>Clients discover groups such as /api/v1 and /apis/apps/v1 before working with objects.</p><pre data-capability-pre>{'/api/v1\n/apis/apps/v1\n/apis/metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1\n/apis/example.io/v1'}</pre></div><div class="api-capability-static" aria-label="API responsibility reading path">{capabilities.flatMap((category)=>category.items).map(([title,copy,example])=><details><summary>{title}</summary><p>{copy}</p><pre>{example}</pre></details>)}</div><details><summary>Show direct-call exceptions</summary><ul><li>API server → kubelet: Pod logs, attach and port-forward.</li><li>API server → matched admission/authentication/authorization webhook.</li><li>API server → aggregated API server for registered extension paths.</li><li>Cloud, CSI, Gateway and Operator controllers → external providers where configured.</li></ul></details></div>}

{view.id==='one-picture' && <div class="api-finale-lab" data-api-finale-lab><div class="api-finale-model"><div><span>HYPOTHETICAL DIRECT COUPLING</span><del>controller ↔ scheduler ↔ kubelet ↔ etcd ↔ every extension</del><small>Not normal Kubernetes state coordination</small></div><div><span>API-CENTERED COORDINATION</span><strong>INTENT → API STATE → LOOPS → NODE REALITY → STATUS ↺</strong><small>Shared objects, policy, watches and independent scope</small></div></div><button type="button" data-finale-step>Reveal the repeated pattern</button><p data-finale-result aria-live="polite">Kubernetes is a distributed system of reconciliation loops centered around a shared, versioned API.</p><details><summary>Show API objects (grouped resource inventory)</summary><div class="api-objects-grid"><div><strong>Workloads</strong><p>Deployments · ReplicaSets · StatefulSets · DaemonSets · Jobs · CronJobs · Pods</p></div><div><strong>Coordination & Discovery</strong><p>Nodes · Leases · Services · EndpointSlices · Ingresses</p></div><div><strong>Configuration & Storage</strong><p>ConfigMaps · Secrets · PersistentVolumeClaims · PersistentVolumes · StorageClasses</p></div><div><strong>Scaling & Policy</strong><p>HorizontalPodAutoscalers · ResourceQuotas · LimitRanges · NetworkPolicies</p></div><div><strong>Auth & Extensibility</strong><p>Roles · RoleBindings · ClusterRoles · ServiceAccounts · CustomResourceDefinitions · APIServices</p></div></div></details><details><summary>Check the seven tempting shortcuts</summary><ul class="api-misconceptions"><li><strong>kubectl is Kubernetes.</strong><span>kubectl is one API client.</span></li><li><strong>Scheduler starts Pods.</strong><span>Scheduler records placement; kubelet executes assigned PodSpecs.</span></li><li><strong>Controllers command kubelets.</strong><span>They normally coordinate indirectly through API state.</span></li><li><strong>Every component uses etcd directly.</strong><span>Ordinary Kubernetes clients use the API server.</span></li><li><strong>WATCH means continuous etcd polling.</strong><span>API watches plus server and client caches form a reactive path.</span></li><li><strong>All network traffic uses kube-apiserver.</strong><span>Ordinary workload traffic follows the data plane.</span></li><li><strong>An API outage instantly kills running containers.</strong><span>Existing node workloads can continue while coordination is impaired.</span></li></ul></details><div class="api-operational-loop"><strong>WHEN THE CLUSTER STALLS</strong><ol><li>Check API endpoint availability, latency and audit evidence.</li><li>Compare object spec with status and the last changed resourceVersion.</li><li>Inspect controller progress, pending scheduling and Node/Lease freshness.</li><li>Check runtime, Service dataplane and storage separately from API state.</li><li>Rehearse recovery of the first broken dependency, then watch loops converge.</li></ol></div></div>}
